    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\nCompiling Cython's `FlowControl.py` module results in non-deterministic C code results due to type inference effects. The following (incomplete) code in line 576+ (in `check_definitions()`) may result in `i_state` being inferred as Python `object` or `int`, resulting in more or less specific code being generated. Since `stat.bit` is an undeclared attribute, `object` is correct here.\n```python\n    block: ControlBlock\n        i_state = block.i_input\n            i_assmts = flow.assmts[stat.entry]\n                i_state = i_state & ~i_assmts.mask\n                if stat.is_deletion:\n                    i_state |= i_assmts.bit  # i_assmts.bit is typed as Python int\n                else:\n                    i_state |= stat.bit  # stat.bit is unknown => object\n```\nThe reason is likely the resolution of circular dependencies, which might succeed or not based on previous inference state, so ordering currently matters.\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour:\n\n_No response_\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\n_No response_\n\n### OS\n\n_No response_\n\n### Python version\n\n_No response_\n\n### Cython version\n\nmaster and probably earlier versions.\n\n### Additional context\n\nI failed to reproduce this myself so far, but it was reported in https://github.com/cython/cython/issues/7997#issuecomment-5695371023",
